<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Gen Physiol</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of General Physiology</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1295</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-7748</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">5693167</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2225836</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>The Ionic Basis of Electrical Activity in Embryonic Cardiac Muscle</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Yeh</surname><given-names>Billy K.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Hoffman</surname><given-names>Brian F.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x1d8f990N0x3429760">From the Department of Pharmacology, College of Physicians and Surgeons, Columbia University, New York 10032.</aff><author-notes><fn><p>Dr. Yeh's present address is the Departments of Pharmacology and Medicine, Emory University School of Medicine, Atlanta, Georgia 30303</p></fn></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>1</day><month>10</month><year>1968</year></pub-date><volume>52</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>666</fpage><lpage>681</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>6</day><month>12</month><year>1967</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1968 by The Rockefeller University Press</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> The intracellular sodium concentration reported for young, embryonic chick hearts is extremely high and decreases progressively throughout the embryonic period, reaching a value of 43 m<sc>M</sc> immediately before hatching. This observation suggested that the ionic basis for excitation in embryonic chick heart may differ from that responsible for electrical activity of the adult organ. This hypothesis was tested by recording transmembrane resting and action potentials on hearts isolated from 6-day and 19-day chick embryos and varying the extracellular sodium and potassium concentrations. The results show that for both young and old embryonic cardiac cells the resting potential depends primarily on the extracellular potassium concentration and the amplitude and rate of rise of the action potential depend primarily on the extracellular sodium concentration. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>